-- ============================================================================
-- Golden Oven — consolidated database schema (migrations 001–039)
--
-- WHAT THIS IS
--   The complete final state of every migration in supabase/migrations/,
--   flattened into one script. Generated by applying all 36 migration files in
--   order to an empty PostgreSQL 16 database and dumping the result, so it is
--   the schema those migrations actually produce — not a hand-transcription.
--   Redundant history is gone: create_order(), for example, is defined once
--   here instead of the eight times it is rewritten across the migrations.
--
-- WHEN TO USE IT
--   Standing up a NEW Supabase project (e.g. moving to a different account).
--   Paste the whole file into the SQL Editor and Run. It replaces running
--   001–039 by hand.
--
-- WHAT IT CONTAINS
--   Structure only — tables, columns, constraints, indexes, sequences,
--   functions/RPCs, row-level-security policies, grants, and the two storage
--   buckets with their policies.
--
-- WHAT IT DOES *NOT* CONTAIN
--   Rows. No products, orders, packages, delivery tiers, site settings or ops
--   data. That is deliberate: you are importing real data from the old
--   project, and pre-seeded reference rows would collide with it on import.
--   • Migrating an existing project → run this, then import your data
--     (pg_dump --data-only), then copy the storage FILES separately (a SQL
--     dump moves the bucket rows, never the files themselves).
--   • Starting genuinely fresh instead → run this, then supabase/seed.sql and
--     supabase/content.sql for demo catalogue + editable-content rows.
--
-- ASSUMPTIONS
--   A managed Supabase project, which already provides the `auth` and
--   `storage` schemas and the anon / authenticated / service_role roles.
--   Safe to run top-to-bottom exactly once on an empty project.
--
-- VERIFIED
--   Applied to a pristine PostgreSQL 16 database, then compared object-by-object
--   against a database built by replaying all 36 migrations:
--     • 202 table columns  — identical
--     • 19 functions       — identical
--     • 36 indexes         — identical
--     • 59 constraints     — identical (incl. the delivery_slot and
--                            slab_letter_max_chars checks)
--     • policies           — identical, plus the two product-images storage
--                            policies, which live in storage-setup.sql rather
--                            than in a migration and are folded in here.
--   Then smoke-tested on the result: create_order() places an order with a
--   delivery slot, lookup_order() returns it, and anon/authenticated hold the
--   execute grants the storefront needs.
-- ============================================================================

-- uuid_generate_v4() backs most primary keys.
create extension if not exists "uuid-ossp";


-- ============================================================================
-- PUBLIC SCHEMA — tables, functions, RLS policies, grants
-- ============================================================================

-- Functions are created before the tables they read (is_admin() and
-- can_view_ops() both query `profiles`). Deferring body validation lets them be
-- defined in any order; Postgres still resolves them correctly at call time.
set check_function_bodies = false;

\restrict yVAxHxeteQh6057YPAvnZ4WJUoMxENbsRroP9zCCtUT8sXiZ6mIxXXXNYnahFgU



